# How to use this guide {.unnumbered}

This guide condenses everything in the project folder: nine lecture decks (Weeks 1–5), four TA sessions, the TA midterm review, the TA practice problems, Problem Sets 1–4 with hints and solutions, and the official formula sheet. Every number in the worked examples was recomputed independently, and a handful of small errors in the source materials are flagged where they appear (and collected in @sec-errata).

The midterm is pen, paper, and calculator only, so the guide emphasizes things you can do by hand. Each section follows the same rhythm:

1. **Core ideas** in prose, with the intuition the professor stresses.
2. **Formulas**, each tagged **[on sheet]** if it appears on the official "Useful Formulas for 35000" sheet, or **[know it]** if it does not.
3. **Worked examples** drawn from the problem sets, lectures, and TA sessions.
4. **Self-test** questions with collapsed answers. Try each one before you click.

Source tags look like this: **L2 s.59** means Lecture Week 2, slide 59 (the number printed on the slide). The abbreviations are:

| Tag | Source | Tag | Source |
|:--|:--|:--|:--|
| L1 | Lecture 1: Risk and Return | TA1–TA4 | TA Sessions 1–4 |
| L2 | Lecture 2: Asset Allocation & Mean-Variance | TAR | TA Midterm Review |
| L3a | Lecture 3: Mean-Variance Continued (HMC case) | PP | Practice Problems from TA Sessions |
| L3b | Lecture 3: The CAPM | PS1–PS4 | Problem Sets 1–4 (and solutions) |
| L4a | Lecture 4: CAPM Continued | FS | Official formula sheet |
| L4b | Lecture 4: Anomalies, Multifactor Models, APT | | |
| L5a | Lecture 5: Multifactor Review & Performance Evaluation | | |
| L5b | Lecture 5: Market Efficiency | | |

## Exam logistics {.unnumbered}

The midterm is in class during Week 6 (November 3/4), in the first 1.5 hours, written in a blue book. You may bring a calculator, and a formula sheet ("cheat sheet") is posted on Canvas (L5a s.2). The syllabus says exam questions "will be similar to the assigned homework problems," and the TA asks you to show your calculations so you can earn partial credit (TA2 s.4). The midterm is optional under grading Method B, so it can only help you.

::: {.callout-note title="About the cheat sheet"}
The posted sheet is the "Useful Formulas for 35000" file in this project. The TA occasionally says things like "write this down on your cheat sheet" (TA2 s.13), which suggests you may be allowed to annotate it. Confirm the exact rule with the TA. Either way, the formulas tagged **[know it]** in this guide are *not* on the official sheet, and @sec-offsheet collects them on one page.
:::

## The course in one picture {.unnumbered}

Every topic in the first half of the course is an answer to one question: **what discount rate (expected return) should an asset have, and why?**

```{mermaid}
flowchart LR
  A["PV formula<br/>P = Σ E[D] / (1+r)^t<br/>(Week 1)"] --> B["r = r_f + risk premium<br/>What is risk?"]
  B --> C["Mean-variance analysis<br/>risk = σ of your portfolio<br/>(Week 2–3)"]
  C --> D["CAPM<br/>risk = β with the market<br/>(Week 3–4)"]
  D --> E["Multifactor models<br/>risk = βs on several factors<br/>(Week 4–5)"]
  E --> F["Market efficiency<br/>Is α real? Joint hypothesis<br/>(Week 5)"]
  D -.-> F
```

## What is on the formula sheet (and what is not) {.unnumbered}

The official sheet covers the "machinery" but leaves out several things that show up constantly in the problem sets and TA questions. The table below is the most useful thing to internalize before the exam.

| Topic | On the sheet **[on sheet]** | Not on the sheet **[know it]** |
|:--|:--|:--|
| Returns | — | HPR with dividends, gross returns, compounding, arithmetic vs. geometric averages, real returns |
| Scenario statistics | — | $E[r]$, variance, SD, covariance, correlation from a probability table |
| Valuation | PV with horizon $n$, Gordon growth (exact and approximate) | Implied return $r = D_1/P + g$; perpetuity $C/r$; annuity; APR vs. EAR |
| One risky + risk-free | $E[r_p]$, $\sigma_p$, Sharpe ratio, $w^*$ | The utility function $U = E - \gamma\sigma^2$ itself; CAL equation; targeting a given $\sigma$ or $E$ |
| Two risky assets | $E[r_p]$, $\sigma_p^2$, MVE weight | **MVP weight**; zero-risk weight when $\rho=-1$; 3-asset variance; covariance of two portfolios |
| Complete portfolio | — | Total-wealth weights $= y \times w^{MVE}$ |
| CAPM | $\beta$, portfolio $\beta$, SML, realized-return regression, $\alpha$ | $\beta = \rho\,\sigma_i/\sigma_m$; $R^2 = \beta^2\sigma_m^2/\sigma_i^2 = \rho^2$; variance decomposition; market $\beta = 1$ |
| Multifactor | Realized and expected return equations, $\alpha$ | Interpreting SMB/HML loadings; annualizing monthly $\alpha$ ($\times 12$) |
| Performance | — | Treynor ratio, information ratio |



# Week 1 — Risk, return, and the asset pricing formula {#sec-week1}

The first week sets up the language of the whole course. Prices are discounted expected cash flows, the discount rate is the risk-free rate plus a risk premium, and the rest of the quarter is about pinning down that risk premium (L1 s.20, s.39).

## Measuring returns

The holding period return is the payoff tomorrow minus the price paid, divided by the price paid (L1 s.22). **[know it]**

$$
r_{t+1} = \frac{P_{t+1} + D_{t+1} - P_t}{P_t}, \qquad R_{t+1} = 1 + r_{t+1} \text{ (gross return)}
$$

$D_{t+1}$ is any cash payout: dividends, coupons, rent. The **expected** (ex ante) return uses $E[\tilde P_{t+1}]$ and $E[\tilde D_{t+1}]$; the **realized** (ex post) return uses what actually happened. They can be very different (L1 s.25).

*Example (L1 s.23).* NVIDIA closed 2024 at \$134.29, closed 2025 at \$186.50, and paid \$0.04 in dividends: $r = (186.50 + 0.04 - 134.29)/134.29 \approx 38.9\%$.

### Compounding and averaging

Multi-period returns compound: $r_{t,t+2} = (1 + r_{t+1})(1 + r_{t+2}) - 1$. Earning 10% then 20% gives 32% over two years, not 30% (L1 s.27). **[know it]**

| | Arithmetic average | Geometric average |
|:--|:--|:--|
| Formula | $\bar r_a = \frac{1}{n}\sum r_i$ | $\bar r_g = \left[\prod (1 + r_i)\right]^{1/n} - 1$ |
| Answers | "What is the typical one-period return?" | "At what constant rate did wealth compound?" |
| Use for | Expected return over one period | Describing historical performance over many periods |
| Size | Always $\geq$ geometric | Always $\leq$ arithmetic; the gap grows with volatility |

The cleanest illustration (L1 s.29): a stock goes \$100 → \$200 → \$100. Returns are +100% and −50%, so the arithmetic average is 25%, but the geometric average is the sensible 0%. Dell's five returns (−20%, 35%, −25%, 40%, 10%) average 8% arithmetically but only 4.5% geometrically.

*TA1 Q1.* Returns 9%, 7%, −3%, −2%, 5% give an arithmetic mean of 3.2%, a geometric mean of 3.1%, and a 5-year cumulative return of 16.4%.

### Nominal versus real

Real returns measure purchasing power (L1 s.32). **[know it]**

$$
1 + r_{real} = \frac{1 + r_{nominal}}{1 + \pi}, \qquad r_{real} \approx r_{nominal} - \pi
$$

## Measuring risk from scenarios

When you are given states of the world with probabilities, weight by the probabilities. **[know it]**

$$
E[r] = \sum_s p_s r_s, \qquad \sigma^2 = \sum_s p_s \left(r_s - E[r]\right)^2, \qquad \sigma = \sqrt{\sigma^2}
$$

With *historical* data you would instead divide by $N-1$ (or $N$; L3a s.40 says it does not matter much). Do not mix the two: a probability table never uses $N-1$.

*Example (L1 s.35–36).* Intel trades at \$100. Next year it is worth \$135 (p = 0.4), \$105 (p = 0.3), or \$75 (p = 0.3). Returns are 35%, 5%, −25%. Then $E[r] = 0.4(35\%) + 0.3(5\%) + 0.3(-25\%) = 8\%$, $\sigma^2 = 0.0621$, and $\sigma = 24.9\%$.

::: {.callout-warning title="Exam trap: the dividend belongs in every state"}
In PS1 Q1 the \$2 dividend is paid with certainty, so it is added to the price in *every* state before computing the return. In the Ford question (TAR Q4, PP Q2) the dividend differs by state. Either way, compute the state return first, then take expectations.
:::

### Why risk earns a premium

People are risk averse: offered a coin flip between +\$50,000 and −\$20,000 or a sure \$15,000 (same expected value), most take the sure thing (L1 s.38). To hold risky assets they must expect a higher return. Historically, from 1926 to 2025, average real returns and standard deviations were 0.3% and 3.8% for T-bills, 1.5% and 8.9% for T-bonds, and 6.8% and 19.3% for stocks (L1 s.37). The two fundamental questions of finance follow (L1 s.39): **what is risk, and how much extra expected return compensates for it?**

## The asset pricing (present value) formula

Rearranging the definition of a return and taking expectations gives the one-period pricing equation, and substituting forward gives the full formula (L1 s.41–45). **[on sheet]**

$$
P_t = E\left[\frac{\tilde D_{t+1} + \tilde P_{t+1}}{1 + r}\right] = E\left[\frac{\tilde D_{t+1}}{1+r} + \frac{\tilde D_{t+2}}{(1+r)^2} + \cdots + \frac{\tilde D_{t+n}}{(1+r)^n} + \frac{\tilde P_{t+n}}{(1+r)^n}\right]
$$

The discount rate $r$ goes by three names: discount rate, expected return, and required return. It decomposes as $r = r_f + \text{risk premium}$ (L1 s.42).

::: {.callout-important title="The three channels (L1 s.46)"}
If a price moves, it must be because of (1) a change in expected cash flows, (2) a change in the risk-free rate, or (3) a change in the risk premium. The risk premium itself can change because the *amount* of risk changed or because the *price* of risk (risk aversion) changed. Almost every "why did prices move?" question is answered by naming the channel.
:::

### Gordon growth model

With constant growth $g$ and constant discount rate $r > g$ (L1 s.47): **[on sheet]**

$$
P_t = \frac{E_t[D_{t+1}]}{r - g} = \frac{D_t(1+g)}{r-g} \approx \frac{D_t}{r-g}
$$

Rearranged to back out the market's expected return: **[know it]**

$$
r = \frac{D_t(1+g)}{P_t} + g = \frac{D_{t+1}}{P_t} + g
$$

The TA computes both the exact version (using $D_{t+1}$) and the approximate version (using $D_t$). Use the exact version unless told otherwise, and say which you used.

A high price-dividend ratio means either high expected growth or a low discount rate. In the late-1990s bubble the P/D ratio was about 90, so $D/P \approx 1.1\%$ (L1 s.49). With $r_f \approx 3\%$, a historical risk premium of 6%, and historical growth of 6.5%, the numbers cannot all hold at once: either growth expectations rose to $g = 3\% + 6\% - 1.1\% = 7.9\%$, or the risk premium fell to $r^e = 1.1\% + 6.5\% - 3\% = 4.6\%$.

### Case study: the COVID crash

The S&P 500 fell from 3,370 to 2,229 (−34%) between February 20 and March 23, 2020 (L1 s.52). Interest rates fell, so channel 2 cannot explain the drop. If dividends were cut 50% for five years and then recovered, the lost value of those first five dividends is only about \$137, far less than the \$1,100 drop (L1 s.55–58). Dividend futures show that the value of *distant* dividends fell most (L1 s.60–61). The lesson is that the stock market is about the long run: long-run growth and discount rates dominate the price (L1 s.64).

## Two-stage growth: the classic exam calculation

This appears in PS1 Q3(b), TA1 Q4, PP Q11, and TAR Q13. The recipe:

1. Write out the dividends during the high-growth years: $D_{t+k} = D_t(1+g_1)^k$.
2. At the switch date $T$, value the rest with Gordon using the *new* growth rate: $P_T = D_T(1+g_2)/(r - g_2)$.
3. Discount everything back. The terminal price $P_T$ is discounted $T$ periods, the same as $D_T$, not $T+1$.

$$
P_t = \sum_{k=1}^{T}\frac{D_{t+k}}{(1+r)^k} + \frac{P_{t+T}}{(1+r)^T}
$$

```{mermaid}
flowchart LR
  A["Years 1..T<br/>D grows at g₁"] --> B["Terminal price at T<br/>P_T = D_T(1+g₂)/(r−g₂)"]
  B --> C["Discount D₁..D_T and P_T<br/>(P_T by (1+r)^T)"]
  C --> D["Sum = P₀"]
```

### Worked: PS1 Q3 and Q4 (answers from TA2 s.7–10, recomputed)

*Q3(a).* $D_0 = \$58$, $r = 8\%$, $g = 5\%$: exact $P = 58(1.05)/0.03 = \$2{,}030$; approximate $58/0.03 = \$1{,}933.33$.

*Q3(b).* 5% growth for 5 years, then 3%. Dividends: \$60.90, \$63.95, \$67.14, \$70.50, \$74.02. Terminal price $P_5 = 74.02(1.03)/0.05 = \$1{,}524.90$. Their present value: $\$266.71 + \$1{,}524.90/1.08^5 = \$266.71 + \$1{,}037.82 = \$1{,}304.53$. (The TA's \$1,304.20 comes from rounding the dividends. With the approximate formula, $P_5 = \$1{,}480.49$ and $P_0 = \$1{,}274.30$.)

*Q4(a).* Market at \$3,000 paying \$60, $g = 4\%$: $r = 60(1.04)/3000 + 0.04 = 6.08\%$ (approximate: 6%).

*Q4(b).* Market drops to \$2,000 with $g$ unchanged: $r = 60(1.04)/2000 + 0.04 = 7.12\%$ (approximate: 7%).

*Q4(c).* With cash-flow expectations unchanged, the discount rate must have risen. That is channel 2 or 3: a higher risk-free rate, more perceived risk, or higher risk aversion. Expected returns *going forward* are now higher.

*TA1 Q4 / PP Q11.* $D_0 = \$70$, $r = 10\%$. Constant 8% growth: $70(1.08)/0.02 = \$3{,}780$. With 8% for 3 years then 3%: dividends \$75.60, \$81.65, \$88.18; $P_3 = 88.18(1.03)/0.07 = \$1{,}297.50$; $P_0 = \$1{,}177.29$.

### Worked: PS1 Q1 and Q2 (no posted solution, computed here)

*Q1.* Bought at \$100, certain \$2 dividend, prices \$80/\$110/\$130 with probabilities 30/40/30%. Returns: −18%, 12%, 32%. $E[r] = 9.0\%$, $\sigma^2 = 0.0381$, $\sigma = 19.52\%$.

*Q2.* Probabilities 20/50/30%. Stock A (−5%, 6%, 10%): $E = 5.0\%$, $\sigma = 5.29\%$. Stock B (2%, 5%, 12%): $E = 6.5\%$, $\sigma = 3.77\%$. B has a higher mean *and* lower risk, so B mean-variance dominates A. (Bonus: their covariance is 0.00165 and correlation 0.83.)

*More scenario practice.* Ford (TAR Q4, PP Q2): returns 66.7% and −13.3%, $E = 34.7\%$, $\sigma^2 = 0.1536$, $\sigma = 39.2\%$. Tesla (TA1 Q2): $E = 0.81\%$, $\sigma = 8.37\%$.

## Compounding frequency, annuities, perpetuities

All three are **[know it]**.

A rate "12% per year compounded monthly" means 1% per month. The effective annual rate is $(1 + APR/m)^m - 1$. A 20% APR credit card compounded monthly costs $(1 + 0.20/12)^{12} - 1 = 21.9\%$ per year (L1 s.70). Present values adjust the same way: $PV = CF_T/(1 + r/m)^{mT}$.

$$
\text{Annuity: } P = \frac{C}{r}\left[1 - \frac{1}{(1+r)^T}\right], \qquad \text{Perpetuity: } P = \frac{C}{r}
$$

\$1,000 a year for 20 years at 5% is worth \$12,462.21; paid monthly (\$83.33) at 5%/12 it is worth about \$12,627 (L1 s.73). A \$3,000 perpetuity discounted at 8.4% is worth \$35,714.29 (TAR Q8).

## Leverage arithmetic (TA1 Q3)

Weights are always fractions of *your own* money. Investor X has \$2,000, lends \$500, and puts \$1,500 in the market: weights 75%/25%, return $0.75(11\%) + 0.25(4\%) = 9.25\%$. Investor Y has \$500, borrows \$500, and invests \$1,000: weights 200%/−100%, return $2(11\%) - 1(4\%) = 18\%$.

## Self-test: Week 1

**1.** You buy at \$50, receive a \$1.50 dividend, and sell at \$54. What is your HPR?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Answer"}
$(54 + 1.50 - 50)/50 = 11\%$.
:::

**2.** Returns are +50%, −30%, +20%. Compute the arithmetic and geometric averages. Which describes how your wealth grew?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Answer"}
Arithmetic: 13.33%. Geometric: $(1.5 \times 0.7 \times 1.2)^{1/3} - 1 = 1.26^{1/3} - 1 = 8.01\%$. The geometric average describes wealth growth.
:::

**3.** A stock trades at \$40, just paid \$2, and dividends grow 4% forever. What expected return is the market pricing in?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Answer"}
$r = 2(1.04)/40 + 0.04 = 9.2\%$ (approximate: 9%).
:::

**4.** A stock falls 20% on a day with no news about its cash flows. What must have changed, and what does it imply for future returns?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Answer"}
The discount rate rose (higher risk-free rate, more risk, or more risk aversion). Higher discount rates mean higher expected returns from here on.
:::

**5.** An 18% APR compounded monthly: what is the effective annual rate?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Answer"}
$(1.015)^{12} - 1 = 19.56\%$.
:::

**6.** Nominal return 8%, inflation 3%. Real return, exact and approximate?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Answer"}
Exact: $1.08/1.03 - 1 = 4.85\%$. Approximate: 5%.
:::


# Week 2 — Asset allocation and mean-variance analysis {#sec-week2}

Markowitz's question: how much of your wealth should go into each security? Two ingredients answer it: how much risk to take, and how to combine risky assets optimally (L2 s.8).

## Utility and the one-risky-asset problem

The course uses a quadratic utility "score" (L2 s.13): **[know it]**

$$
U(E, \sigma) = E - \gamma \sigma^2
$$

Higher $\gamma$ means more risk averse; $\gamma = 0$ means risk neutral.

::: {.callout-warning title="Convention alert"}
The textbook (BKM) writes $U = E - \tfrac{1}{2}A\sigma^2$. This course has **no ½**, which is why the optimal weight on the formula sheet has $2\gamma$ in the denominator. Always use the course's version.
:::

*Example (L2 s.14, s.18).* With $\gamma = 2$: T-bills (5.5%, 0%) score 0.055; the S&P 500 (7.91%, 12.1%) scores $0.0791 - 2(0.121)^2 = 0.0498$; a 60/40 mix (7.0%, 7.26%) scores 0.0595, better than either extreme.

### The capital allocation line (CAL)

Put $w$ in a risky asset and $1-w$ in the risk-free asset: **[on sheet]**

$$
E[r_p] = w E[r_A] + (1-w) r_f = r_f + w\left(E[r_A] - r_f\right), \qquad \sigma_p = w \sigma_A
$$

Eliminating $w$ gives the CAL, a straight line in $(\sigma, E)$ space whose slope is the Sharpe ratio (L2 s.26, s.73): **[CAL equation: know it; Sharpe ratio: on sheet]**

$$
E[r_p] = r_f + \underbrace{\frac{E[r_A] - r_f}{\sigma_A}}_{\text{Sharpe ratio}}\,\sigma_p
$$

Every point on a CAL has the **same Sharpe ratio** as the risky asset that defines it (PS2 Q3; PP Q3).

### The optimal weight

Maximize $r_f + w(E[r_A] - r_f) - \gamma w^2 \sigma_A^2$. The first-order condition $E[r_A] - r_f - 2\gamma w \sigma_A^2 = 0$ gives (L2 s.21): **[on sheet]**

$$
w^* = \frac{E[r_A] - r_f}{2\gamma\sigma_A^2}
$$

You hold more of the risky asset when its risk premium is higher, less when it is riskier, and less when you are more risk averse (L2 s.22). In the example, $w^* = (0.0791 - 0.055)/(2 \cdot 2 \cdot 0.121^2) = 41\%$; with $\gamma = 4$ it halves to 21%. $w^* > 1$ means borrowing at $r_f$; $w^* < 0$ means shorting the risky asset.

### Targeting a return or a risk level

Exam questions often fix a target instead of $\gamma$. **[know it]**

$$
\text{Target } \sigma^*: \; w = \frac{\sigma^*}{\sigma_A}, \qquad \text{Target } E^*: \; w = \frac{E^* - r_f}{E[r_A] - r_f}
$$

*PS2 Q3(a).* Target 10% with fund A (8%, 20%) and $r_f = 2\%$: $w = (10 - 2)/(8 - 2) = 133.3\%$, so you borrow \$333,333 on \$1 million. $\sigma = 26.67\%$ and Sharpe $= 0.30$, identical to fund A's. With fund B (12%, 30%): $w = 80\%$, $\sigma = 24\%$, Sharpe $= 0.333$. B is preferred: same expected return, less risk, because B has the higher Sharpe ratio.

### Three-stage analysis and indifference curves

The general method (L2 s.24–28): (1) estimate inputs (means, variances, covariances; the means are the hardest), (2) map the investment opportunity set, (3) pick the point that maximizes utility.

Indifference curves connect portfolios with equal utility; utility rises toward the upper left. More risk-averse investors have steeper curves. The optimal portfolio is where the highest attainable indifference curve just touches the CAL (L2 s.29–35). Vocabulary (L2 s.33): an investment is *mean-variance preferred* if it has higher $E$ **and** lower $\sigma$, *mean-variance dominated* if it has lower $E$ **and** higher $\sigma$; "preferred" and "dominated" alone refer to higher or lower indifference curves and depend on the investor.

## Portfolio mathematics

Expected returns are always weighted averages (L2 s.38). Covariance and correlation measure co-movement (L2 s.39–42): **[know it]**

$$
\text{cov}(r_1, r_2) = \sigma_{1,2} = \sum_s p_s (r_{1,s} - E[r_1])(r_{2,s} - E[r_2]), \qquad \rho_{1,2} = \frac{\sigma_{1,2}}{\sigma_1 \sigma_2} \in [-1, 1]
$$

Two-asset variance **[on sheet]**:

$$
\sigma_p^2 = w_1^2\sigma_1^2 + w_2^2\sigma_2^2 + 2w_1w_2\sigma_{1,2} = w_1^2\sigma_1^2 + w_2^2\sigma_2^2 + 2w_1w_2\rho_{1,2}\sigma_1\sigma_2
$$

Three or more assets (PS2 hint) **[know it]**: every pair contributes a covariance term, and there are $(N^2 - N)/2$ distinct pairs (TA2 s.11).

$$
\sigma_p^2 = \sum_i \sum_j w_i w_j \sigma_{i,j} = w_1^2\sigma_1^2 + w_2^2\sigma_2^2 + w_3^2\sigma_3^2 + 2w_1w_2\sigma_{1,2} + 2w_1w_3\sigma_{1,3} + 2w_2w_3\sigma_{2,3}
$$

Covariance between two portfolios $a$ and $b$ built from the same assets (PS2 hint) **[know it]**: $\text{cov}(r_a, r_b) = \sum_i \sum_j w_i^a w_j^b \sigma_{i,j}$.

*Example (L2 s.40–42).* IBM and Google across three states (p = 0.8, 0.1, 0.1): $E = 11\%$ and $13\%$, $\text{cov} = 0.0357$, $\sigma = 19.2\%$ and $26.5\%$, so $\rho = 0.70$.

*Example (L2 s.44).* $\sigma_1 = 30\%$, $\sigma_2 = 20\%$, covariance 0.0002, equal weights: $\sigma_p^2 = 0.0326$, $\sigma_p = 18.1\%$, below the 25% average of the two.

## Diversification and the role of correlation

With two assets of variance 0.04 each, the equal-weighted variance is $0.02 + 0.02\rho$ (L2 s.48). As long as $\rho < 1$, diversification lowers risk below the weighted average of the individual risks.

```{=html}
<figure style="max-width:680px;margin:1.5em auto;">
<svg viewBox="0 0 640 420" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif" role="img" aria-label="Correlation and the frontier">
<rect x="0" y="0" width="640" height="420" fill="#ffffff"/>
<line x1="70.0" y1="30" x2="70.0" y2="360" stroke="#eeeeee"/><text x="70.0" y="378" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#444">0%</text>
<line x1="178.0" y1="30" x2="178.0" y2="360" stroke="#eeeeee"/><text x="178.0" y="378" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#444">20%</text>
<line x1="286.0" y1="30" x2="286.0" y2="360" stroke="#eeeeee"/><text x="286.0" y="378" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#444">40%</text>
<line x1="394.0" y1="30" x2="394.0" y2="360" stroke="#eeeeee"/><text x="394.0" y="378" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#444">60%</text>
