<line x1="72.7" y1="213.7" x2="102.4" y2="277.5" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="1"/>
<text x="105.1" y="288.5" font-size="12" fill="#c0392b">zero-risk mix: w_A = 25%, E = 13.75%</text>
<rect x="82" y="36" width="300" height="70" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#dddddd"/>
<line x1="90" y1="48" x2="118" y2="48" stroke="#7f8c8d" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="6,4"/><text x="126" y="52" font-size="12" fill="#222">ρ = +1 (no diversification)</text>
<line x1="90" y1="68" x2="118" y2="68" stroke="#1f4e79" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="126" y="72" font-size="12" fill="#222">ρ = 0</text>
<line x1="90" y1="88" x2="118" y2="88" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="126" y="92" font-size="12" fill="#222">ρ = −1 (perfect hedge possible)</text>
</svg>
<figcaption style="font-size:0.9em;color:#555;margin-top:0.4em;">Long-only combinations of A (25%, 75%) and B (10%, 25%) under three correlations (L2 s.49–55). Lower correlation bends the frontier further left.</figcaption>
</figure>
```

The three cases (L2 s.49–55), using A (25%, 75%) and B (10%, 25%):

- **$\rho = +1$**: the frontier is a straight line, so there is no diversification benefit.
- **$\rho = 0$**: the frontier bows to the left, so some mixes have less risk than B alone.
- **$\rho = -1$**: a perfect hedge exists. Setting $\sigma_p = |w\sigma_A - (1-w)\sigma_B| = 0$ gives **[know it]** $w_A = \sigma_B/(\sigma_A + \sigma_B) = 0.25$, with $E = 13.75\%$ and zero risk.

::: {.callout-important title="Zero risk must earn the risk-free rate"}
If two assets with $\rho = -1$ can be combined into a riskless portfolio, that portfolio must earn $r_f$, or there is an arbitrage. TA2 Q2: Tesla (26%, 50%) and IBM (6%, 25%) with $\rho = -1$ give $w_{Tesla} = 1/3$ and $E = 12.67\%$, so $r_f$ must be 12.67%. TA3 Q7: stocks and crypto mixed 40/60 return 2% in both states; if T-bills pay 4%, short the 2% portfolio and buy T-bills for a riskless profit.
:::

### Systematic versus diversifiable risk

For an equally weighted portfolio of $N$ stocks, the $N$-asset formula collapses to

$$
\sigma_p^2 = \frac{1}{N}\,\overline{\sigma^2} + \left(1 - \frac{1}{N}\right)\overline{\text{cov}} \;\longrightarrow\; \overline{\text{cov}} \text{ as } N \to \infty
$$

The variances wash out but the average covariance stays. For NYSE stocks the average standard deviation is 49%, the average covariance 0.037, and the average correlation about 39% (L2 s.91–92), so even a huge portfolio keeps roughly $\sqrt{0.037} \approx 19\%$ volatility. That floor is **systematic** risk; what disappears is **diversifiable** (non-systematic) risk. In large portfolios, covariance matters, not variance (L2 s.93). Total risk falls at a decreasing rate as you add stocks (TA3 Q1).

### Covariance is marginal risk

The appendix result that sets up the CAPM (L2 s.96–99): **[know it, conceptually]**

$$
\frac{\partial\, \text{var}(r_p)}{\partial w_j} = 2\,\text{cov}(r_j, r_p)
$$

Adding a little of asset $j$ raises portfolio variance if $j$ covaries positively with the portfolio and lowers it if negatively. That is why, in TA3 Q3, adding stock Z (covariance −0.5 with your holding) cuts risk more than adding Y (+0.5). At the minimum-variance portfolio you cannot reduce variance by tilting, which happens exactly when $\text{cov}(r_p, r_A) = \text{cov}(r_p, r_B)$.

### Case study: stock–bond correlation (L2 s.62–68)

From 2010–2019 the SPY–AGG correlation was −0.23; from 2020 to September 2026 it was +0.52. When stocks and bonds move together, bonds diversify less and the frontier shifts right. Correlations are not constant across regimes, which is a problem for any analysis that treats them as known.

## The minimum variance portfolio (MVP)

The lowest-variance combination of the risky assets (no risk-free asset involved). Minimizing $\sigma_p^2$ over $w_1$ gives (L2 s.59–60): **[know it]** — the TA says "make sure to memorize the last line" (TA2 s.12)

$$
w_1^{MVP} = \frac{\sigma_2^2 - \sigma_{1,2}}{\sigma_1^2 + \sigma_2^2 - 2\sigma_{1,2}}, \qquad w_2^{MVP} = 1 - w_1^{MVP}
$$

*Example (L2 s.60).* $\sigma_1 = 20\%$, $\sigma_2 = 40\%$, $\rho = 0.3$: $\sigma_{1,2} = 0.024$, so $w_1 = (0.16 - 0.024)/(0.04 + 0.16 - 0.048) = 89.47\%$.

The MVP uses only variances and covariances. Expected returns and $r_f$ never enter.

## The mean-variance efficient (tangency) portfolio

With a risk-free asset, the best risky portfolio is the one with the **highest Sharpe ratio**: it gives the steepest CAL (L2 s.74). For two risky assets (L2 s.76): **[on sheet]**

$$
w_B^{MVE} = \frac{E[r_B^e]\,\sigma_C^2 - E[r_C^e]\,\text{cov}(r_B, r_C)}{E[r_B^e]\,\sigma_C^2 + E[r_C^e]\,\sigma_B^2 - \left(E[r_B^e] + E[r_C^e]\right)\text{cov}(r_B, r_C)}, \qquad r^e = r - r_f
$$

::: {.callout-warning title="Exam trap: excess returns"}
Plug in **excess** returns $E[r] - r_f$, not raw returns. This is the single most common slip in PS2. Also convert $\rho$ to covariance: $\text{cov} = \rho\sigma_B\sigma_C$.
:::

*Example (L2 s.70–76).* B (10%, 20%), C (15%, 30%), $\rho = 0.5$, $r_f = 3\%$. Excess returns 7% and 12%; covariance 0.03. $w_B = (0.07 \cdot 0.09 - 0.12 \cdot 0.03)/(0.07 \cdot 0.09 + 0.12 \cdot 0.04 - 0.19 \cdot 0.03) = 0.50$. Then $E_{MVE} = 12.5\%$, $\sigma_{MVE} = 21.79\%$, Sharpe $= 0.436$, beating B alone (0.35) and C alone (0.40).

