### Many assets: two-fund separation

All efficient portfolios are combinations of any two efficient portfolios, so the MVP and the MVE trace out the whole frontier (L2 s.85). In matrix form (L2 s.87):

$$
w^{MV} = \frac{\Sigma^{-1}\mathbf{1}}{\mathbf{1}'\Sigma^{-1}\mathbf{1}}, \qquad w^{MVE} = \frac{\Sigma^{-1}(\mu - r_f\mathbf{1})}{\mathbf{1}'\Sigma^{-1}(\mu - r_f\mathbf{1})}
$$

The slide is explicit: **you will not be asked to solve this with more than two risky assets on the exam** (L2 s.86). Know the idea, use Solver in practice. One conceptual point from L2 s.89–90: adding asset D (5%, 45%) with −0.2 correlation to the others raises the maximum Sharpe ratio from 0.413 to 0.434 despite D's poor stand-alone profile, because its negative correlation is valuable.

## The complete portfolio: putting it together

The key lesson of mean-variance analysis (L2 s.79, s.93): **everyone holds the same risky portfolio (the MVE)**; risk aversion only decides how much of it to hold versus the risk-free asset.

```{mermaid}
flowchart TD
  S1["1. MVE weights<br/>w_B, w_C = 1 − w_B<br/>(use excess returns)"] --> S2["2. MVE statistics<br/>E[r_MVE], σ_MVE"]
  S2 --> S3["3. Fraction y in MVE<br/>y = (E[r_MVE] − r_f) / (2γσ²_MVE)<br/>or y = σ* / σ_MVE, or y = (E* − r_f)/(E[r_MVE] − r_f)"]
  S3 --> S4["4. Total-wealth weights<br/>B: y·w_B, C: y·w_C, risk-free: 1 − y"]
  S4 --> S5["5. Dollars = weight × wealth"]
```

::: {.callout-important title="Risky weights versus total weights (TA2 s.19)"}
$w_A^{MVE}$ is the share of your *risky* money in A, not the share of your *wealth*. Your total weight in A is $y \times w_A^{MVE}$. In TA2 Q3(c), with 60% in the MVE and $w_A^{MVE} = 74\%$, you hold 44.4% of wealth (\$66,600 of \$150,000) in A.
:::

### Worked: PS2 Q2 (Triad funds; also PP Q1)

A is risk-free at 4%; B (19%, 25%); C (10%, 15%); \$200,000 to invest.

*(a)* Match C's 15% risk with A and B: $w_B = 0.15/0.25 = 60\%$, so \$120,000 in B and \$80,000 in A, earning $0.6(19\%) + 0.4(4\%) = 13\%$ versus C's 10%. Same risk, more return.

*(c)* With $\rho_{BC} = 0$: $w_B^{MVE} = (0.15 \cdot 0.0225)/(0.15 \cdot 0.0225 + 0.06 \cdot 0.0625) = 47.4\%$, $w_C = 52.6\%$. $E_{MVE} = 14.26\%$, $\sigma_{MVE} = 14.23\%$, Sharpe 0.72 versus 0.60 (B) and 0.40 (C).

*(d)* Match 15% risk with A and the MVE: $y = 0.15/0.1423 = 105.4\%$. Dollars: risk-free −\$10,786 (borrowing), B \$99,846, C \$110,940. Expected return $4\% + 0.72 \times 15\% = 14.8\%$, beating (a)'s 13% because the MVE's Sharpe ratio (0.72) beats B's (0.60).

*(e)* The efficient set is the CAL through the MVE; every combination on it has the same Sharpe ratio.

### Worked: PS2 Q3(c), recomputed

Funds A (8%, 20%) and B (12%, 30%), $\rho = 0.2$, $r_f = 2\%$, target 10%, \$1 million. Covariance $= 0.012$. $w_B^{MVE} = (0.10 \cdot 0.04 - 0.06 \cdot 0.012)/(0.10 \cdot 0.04 + 0.06 \cdot 0.09 - 0.16 \cdot 0.012) = 0.00328/0.00748 = 43.85\%$, so $w_A = 56.15\%$. $E_{MVE} = 9.75\%$, $\sigma_{MVE} = 18.93\%$, Sharpe 0.41 (vs. 0.30 for A and 0.33 for B). $y = (10 - 2)/(9.75 - 2) = 103.2\%$. Dollars: A \$579,310, B \$452,414, T-bill −\$31,724. Portfolio $\sigma = 19.53\%$, the lowest risk achievable for a 10% target. (The posted solution reports 43.95% and slightly different dollars; see @sec-errata.)

### Worked: TA2 Q3

A (10%, 10%), B (20%, 29%), $\rho = -0.25$, $r_f = 5\%$. $w_A^{MVE} \approx 74\%$, $E_{MVE} \approx 12.6\%$, $\sigma_{MVE} \approx 9.15\%$. To match B's 29% risk: $y = 0.29/0.0915 = 3.17$ (borrow 2.17 times your wealth), giving $E \approx 29.1\%$ versus B's 20%.

## Self-test: Week 2

**1.** Why does every mean-variance investor hold the same mix of risky assets, regardless of risk aversion?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Answer"}
With a risk-free asset available, the steepest CAL (highest Sharpe ratio) dominates every other risky combination. Every investor wants to be on that line; risk aversion only chooses the point on it, i.e., the split between the MVE and the risk-free asset.
:::

**2.** $\sigma_1 = 10\%$, $\sigma_2 = 20\%$, $\rho = 0$. What is the MVP weight on asset 1?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Answer"}
$w_1 = (0.04 - 0)/(0.01 + 0.04 - 0) = 80\%$.
:::

**3.** (TAR Q5 / PP Q3) $r_f = 5\%$. Which security belongs in the complete portfolio? A (15%, $\sigma^2 = 0.04$), B (10%, 0.0225), C (12%, 0.10), D (13%, 0.0625).

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Answer"}
A. Sharpe ratios: A 0.50, B 0.33, C 0.22, D 0.32. Watch out: the table gives **variances**, so take square roots first.
:::

**4.** (TAR Q6 / PP Q4) Which cannot lie on the efficient frontier: A (13%, 31%), B (7%, 10%), C (22%, 54%), D (18%, 22%)?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Answer"}
A. D has a higher expected return and lower standard deviation, so A is mean-variance dominated.
:::

**5.** (TAR Q7 / PP Q5) Your portfolio has $E = 12\%$, $\sigma = 4\%$. Stock XYZ has correlation −1 with it. What can adding XYZ do?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Answer"}
With a carefully chosen weight you can drive risk to zero: $\sigma_p^2 = (w_{XYZ}\sigma_{XYZ} - w_{op}\sigma_{op})^2$, which is zero when $w_{XYZ}\sigma_{XYZ} = w_{op}\sigma_{op}$. Not "any weight."
:::

**6.** (TA2 Q1) \$1,000 of your own, borrow \$1,000 at 5%, split equally between stocks returning 10% and 15%. Expected return?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Answer"}
Weights 100%, 100%, −100%: $10\% + 15\% - 5\% = 20\%$.
:::

**7.** Your complete portfolio is 70% MVE and 30% T-bills. How does its Sharpe ratio compare with the MVE's?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Answer"}
Identical. Any mix of the risk-free asset and one risky portfolio lies on that portfolio's CAL.
:::


# Week 3a — Mean-variance analysis in practice {#sec-mvpractice}

Mean-variance theory is uncontroversial; implementing it is hard (L2 s.94). The Harvard Management Company (HMC) case shows why.

## Getting inputs (L3a s.18–20)

The consensus is that variances and covariances can be estimated reasonably well from history, while **expected returns cannot**: the future may look very different from the past, and the optimal weights are far more sensitive to means than to variances. HMC combined long and short histories, consultant views, current market conditions, and sanity checks across asset classes. Its estimates ranged from cash (3%, 1%) and inflation-indexed bonds (3.6%, 3%) up to emerging markets (8.5%, 20%) and private equity (9.5%, 20%).

## What goes wrong with unconstrained optimization

Unconstrained, HMC's MVE put about 30% in commodities and 18% in inflation-indexed bonds and *shorted* absolute-return funds (about −9%); the minimum variance portfolio was 86% inflation-indexed bonds (L3a s.23). These are not portfolios anyone would hold. The reasons (L3a s.34–37):

- **Sensitivity.** Optimal weights swing wildly with small changes in inputs, especially expected returns.
- **Estimation error.** Britten-Jones (1999) estimated global tangency weights with standard errors: Australia 12.8% (SE 23.6%), Canada −45.2% (SE 38.9%), U.S. 59.3% (SE 47.0%). The 95% interval for Australia runs from roughly −33% to +59%, so we know almost nothing about the true weights.
- **1/N is hard to beat.** DeMiguel, Garlappi, and Uppal (2009): none of 14 optimization models consistently beats equal weighting out of sample across 7 datasets. You would need about 250 years of data with 25 assets (500 years with 50) for sample-based MVE to win.

The fix in practice is to impose constraints: no short sales or margin ($0 \le w_i \le 1$), or policy bands around target weights (L3a s.24–29). HMC's constrained policy portfolio had a Sharpe ratio of about 0.35 (L3a s.31). In step three, practitioners pick a risk level rather than a $\gamma$, using the normal-distribution rule of thumb: returns fall within $E \pm \sigma$ about 68% of the time and $E \pm 2\sigma$ about 95% of the time (L3a s.30).

The same lesson showed up in **PS4 Q3**: the unconstrained MVE of the market, small-cap, and value portfolios was −125.95% market, −31.55% small, and +257.50% value (L5a s.20). The posted solution's advice: these are ex post optimal, but you should not use them going forward. Remedies listed: forbid short sales (which pushed the solution to a corner of 100% value), shrink correlations toward their mean, assume stocks covary only through the market, or shrink weights toward 1/3. The trade-off is that constraints sacrifice in-sample Sharpe ratio in exchange for robustness to estimation error.

## Estimating expected returns (L3a s.38–39)

Averaging the last ten years of returns is not reasonable: the estimate is noisy and backward-looking. A better route is a variable observed today that forecasts returns. The dividend yield, the earnings-price ratio, and some interest-rate variables work. Example regression: $r_t = -0.0932 + 4.46\,(D_{t-1}/P_{t-1}) + \varepsilon_t$, with a slope standard error of 1.70. The t-statistic is $4.46/1.70 = 2.62$; since $|t| \geq 2$, the slope is statistically significant.

Harvard's actual allocation shows how much practice drifted from 1980's 66% public equity and 35% bonds to 2025's 14% public equity, 41% private equity, and 31% hedge funds (L3a s.32).

# Weeks 3–4 — The Capital Asset Pricing Model {#sec-capm}

Mean-variance analysis tells *you* what to hold given inputs. The CAPM asks what happens in equilibrium if *everyone* does it, and the answer pins down every asset's expected return.

## Assumptions and the separation principle

The lecture's two key assumptions (L3b s.3, L4a s.3): **everybody knows what the tangency portfolio is** (everyone uses mean-variance analysis with the same inputs), and **everybody wants to hold it**. The TA's fuller list (TA3 s.7): homogeneous expectations, rational risk-averse utility maximizers, price takers, no transaction costs, and unlimited borrowing and lending at $r_f$.

The separation principle follows: everyone holds risky assets in the same proportions (L3b s.4). If the tangency portfolio is 40% utilities and 60% tech, an aggressive investor holds exactly 40/60, while a cautious one holding 90% T-bills holds 4% utilities and 6% tech. The ratio is always 4:6.

## Deriving the CAPM (L3b s.5–7)

At the tangency portfolio $T$, the reward per unit of marginal risk must be equal across all assets (otherwise you could tilt and raise the Sharpe ratio):

$$
\frac{E[r_i] - r_f}{\text{cov}(r_i, r_T)} = \frac{E[r_j] - r_f}{\text{cov}(r_j, r_T)} = \frac{E[r_T] - r_f}{\text{var}(r_T)}
$$

Solving for $E[r_i]$ needs $T$, which we do not know. Market clearing closes the loop: all shares must be held, so in aggregate investors hold the market portfolio. Since everyone holds $T$, **the tangency portfolio is the market portfolio**. Replacing $T$ with $M$: **[on sheet]**

$$
E[r_i] = r_f + \beta_i\left(E[r_m] - r_f\right), \qquad \beta_i = \frac{\text{cov}(r_i, r_m)}{\text{var}(r_m)}
$$

Read it as $E[r_i] = r_f + \beta_i \times \lambda$: $\beta_i$ is the *amount* of risk, and $\lambda = E[r_m] - r_f$ is the *price* of risk (L4a s.5).

## What is risk in the CAPM?

**Covariance with the market, measured by beta** (L3b s.7–9). Covariance is marginal variance (Week 2's $\partial\text{var}/\partial w_j = 2\text{cov}$), and as in economics, it is the margin that sets prices.

The intuition: high-beta stocks pay off when the market is up, exactly when you need money least, so they must offer high expected returns to be held. An asset with huge variance but zero covariance with the market earns only $r_f$: in a diversified portfolio its variance washes out, so it deserves no compensation. A negative-beta asset acts as insurance and should earn *less* than $r_f$.

